      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>What is a Slitherlink puzzle?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">Slitherlink is a logic puzzle where you draw a single closed loop along the grid lines between dots. A number inside a cell tells you exactly how many of that cell's four edge segments belong to the loop, and the loop never crosses or branches — every dot is touched by either zero or two segments. It was invented by Naoki Inaba and first published by Nikoli in 1989.</div>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>Is Slitherlink the same as Loop the Loop?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">Yes. Slitherlink, Slither Link, Loop the Loop, Fences and Suria no Kirinai are all the exact same puzzle with identical rules — different publishers in the US, UK and Japan simply use different names for the same game.</div>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>Are slitherlink puzzles good for your brain?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">Yes. Slitherlink trains spatial reasoning, pattern recognition and careful deduction. Every clue only constrains its own cell, but the rule that the loop must close forces you to reason about the whole grid at once — a great workout for structured, step-by-step thinking.</div>
      </details>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>Is this Slitherlink puzzle free?</summary>
